+# Contract: HTTP API
+
+**Feature**: `001-pastebin-service` | **Plan**: [../plan.md](../plan.md)
+
+Fixed by the spec's Constraints section — paths, methods, header name, JSON field names, and status
+codes are not implementation choices. All request and response bodies are JSON encoded as UTF-8
+unless stated otherwise. The service binds `127.0.0.1` only.
+
+Every response carries an accurate `Content-Length`. Connections are HTTP/1.0 and close after one
+response ([../research.md](../research.md) R-01).
+
+## Endpoints
+
+### `POST /pastes` — publish a snippet
+
+Request body (a JSON object):
+
+| Field | Type | Required | Meaning |
+|-------|------|----------|---------|
+| `content` | string | yes | The snippet text. ≤ 65,536 bytes UTF-8 encoded. Empty string allowed. |
+| `expires_in` | integer or `null` | no | Lifetime in seconds, `1`…`2592000`. Absent or `null` means never expires. |
+
+Unknown fields are ignored. The `Content-Type` header is not required and is never used to accept or
+reject a request (FR-041).
+
+Response `201 Created`:
+
+```json
+{"id": "Vk3q7mB2x9aLpR4sTzYwQg", "delete_token": "…43 chars…", "url": "/pastes/Vk3q7mB2x9aLpR4sTzYwQg"}
+```
+
+`url` is always exactly `/pastes/<id>`. `delete_token` is disclosed here and nowhere else.
+
+Failures: `400` (body not valid JSON, body not an object, `content` missing or not a string,
+`expires_in` invalid, `Content-Length` unparseable), `413` (`content` over 65,536 encoded bytes, or a
+request body over 394,240 bytes / a declared `Content-Length` over that bound).
+
+### `GET /pastes/<id>` — read a snippet
+
+Response `200 OK`:
+
+```json
+{"id": "Vk3q7mB2x9aLpR4sTzYwQg", "content": "hello", "created_at": "2026-10-23T20:15:00Z", "expires_at": null}
+```
+
+`expires_at` is `null` for a snippet with no lifetime, otherwise an ISO 8601 UTC timestamp with
+second precision and a trailing `Z`. `delete_token` is never present.
+
+Failures: `404` (unknown id, expired, deleted, or an id that is not `^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$`),
+`500` (stored record unreadable or unparseable).
+
+### `GET /pastes/<id>/raw` — read a snippet's text
+
+Response `200 OK` with the snippet's text as the entire body — no wrapper, no added newline — and
+`Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8`. An empty snippet yields an empty body with
+`Content-Length: 0`.
+
+Failures: as `GET /pastes/<id>`.
+
+### `DELETE /pastes/<id>` — withdraw a snippet
+
+Request header `X-Delete-Token: <delete_token>`. No request body.
+
+Response `204 No Content` with an empty body.
+
+Failures:
+- `404` — unknown, expired, or already-deleted id, **regardless of the token presented**. Not-found
+  takes precedence over refusal (FR-026), so the endpoint never reveals the existence of an id that
+  a read would not have revealed.
+- `403` — the snippet exists and is unexpired but `X-Delete-Token` is absent or does not match. The
+  snippet remains readable.
+
+The token comparison uses `secrets.compare_digest`, so it does not leak how much of a presented
+token was correct (FR-025).
+
+## Everything else
+
+Any other path — including `/`, `/pastes/<id>/extra`, `/pastes/<id>/text` — and any defined path used
+with a method it does not support (`GET`/`DELETE`/`PUT`/`PATCH`/`HEAD`/`OPTIONS` on `/pastes`,
+`POST`/`PUT`/`PATCH`/`HEAD`/`OPTIONS` on `/pastes/<id>`) responds `404` with the standard error body.
+A query string is ignored when matching the path.
+
+## Error body
+
+Every unsuccessful response (4xx and 5xx) has exactly this shape (FR-032):
+
+```json
+{"error": "human-readable message naming what was wrong"}
+```
+
+Canonical messages — tests assert the status and that `error` is a non-empty string naming the
+problem, so wording may be refined without breaking the contract:
+
+| Status | Condition | Message |
+|--------|-----------|---------|
+| 400 | body is not valid JSON / not UTF-8 | `request body is not valid JSON` |
+| 400 | body is valid JSON but not an object | `request body must be a JSON object` |
+| 400 | `content` missing or not a string | `'content' is required and must be a string` |
+| 400 | `expires_in` invalid | `'expires_in' must be an integer from 1 to 2592000, or null` |
+| 400 | `Content-Length` not a non-negative integer | `invalid Content-Length header` |
+| 400 | body shorter than declared / connection went quiet | `request body incomplete` |
+| 413 | `content` over the size limit | `content exceeds the maximum of 65536 bytes` |
+| 413 | request body over the read ceiling | `request body exceeds the maximum of 394240 bytes` |
+| 403 | delete token missing or wrong | `missing or invalid X-Delete-Token` |
+| 404 | unknown / expired / deleted paste, or any undefined path or method | `not found` |
+| 500 | stored record unreadable or unparseable | `stored paste could not be read` |
+
+A `500` never discloses a filesystem path or exception text to the caller; the detail goes to the
+service's standard error stream instead ([cli.md](./cli.md)).
+
+## Status codes used
+
+`201`, `200`, `204`, `400`, `403`, `404`, `413`, `500`. No other status is produced by any input this
+specification describes (FR-037). The malformed-HTTP responses `http.server` itself emits (`400` on
+an unparseable request line, `414`, `505`) are outside that set and are reachable only by sending
+something that is not an HTTP request — see [../research.md](../research.md) R-07.
