+# Contract: On-disk storage format
+
+**Feature**: `001-pastebin-service` | **Plan**: [../plan.md](../plan.md)
+
+This is the restart contract (FR-029, FR-030): a later instance of the service started against the
+same directory must read, serve, and delete records written by an earlier one. It is published here
+because tests depend on it to construct expired and damaged records
+([../research.md](../research.md) R-10).
+
+## Layout
+
+```text
+<DIR>/
+├── Vk3q7mB2x9aLpR4sTzYwQg.json     # one live-or-expired snippet
+├── 7pL0nZ4cQw8xKdRm2vYbSa.json
+└── .tmp-aK8xQ2mv                   # transient, exists only during a publication
+```
+
+Flat; no subdirectories. The file name is `<id>.json` where `<id>` is the snippet identifier
+verbatim. A deleted snippet has no file. An expired snippet keeps its file (expiry is lazy) and is
+indistinguishable from a live one on disk — the `expires_at` value decides.
+
+Files whose names do not end in `.json` are ignored; nothing enumerates the directory during normal
+operation anyway, so a stray file cannot affect any request.
+
+## Record
+
+A single JSON object, UTF-8 encoded, with exactly these five keys:
+
+```json
+{
+  "id": "Vk3q7mB2x9aLpR4sTzYwQg",
+  "content": "hello",
+  "created_at": "2026-10-23T20:15:00Z",
+  "expires_at": "2026-10-23T21:15:00Z",
+  "delete_token": "3bW9…"
+}
+```
+
+| Key | Type | Rule |
+|-----|------|------|
+| `id` | string | Matches the file name stem |
+| `content` | string | The snippet text, unmodified |
+| `created_at` | string | `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` |
+| `expires_at` | string or `null` | `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ`, or `null` for never |
+| `delete_token` | string | Non-empty |
+
+A record is **valid** if it decodes as UTF-8 JSON, is an object, has all five keys with the types
+above, `id` equals the file-name stem, and both timestamps parse under that exact format. Anything
+else is **damaged** and yields `500` for that identifier plus a stderr diagnostic (FR-038, FR-039) —
+never `404`. Extra keys are not expected and make the record damaged; nothing the service writes
+produces them.
+
+## Write protocol (publication)
+
+1. Serialise the record.
+2. Create `<DIR>/.tmp-<random>` exclusively, write the bytes, `flush`, `os.fsync`, close.
+3. `os.link(tmp, "<DIR>/<id>.json")` — atomic; raises `FileExistsError` if the identifier is already
+   in use, in which case a fresh identifier is generated and the attempt repeats (up to 5 times).
+4. `os.unlink(tmp)`.
+5. Only then is the `201` response sent.
+
+A reader therefore sees `<id>.json` either absent or complete, never partial (FR-043). If the process
+dies between steps 2 and 3, a `.tmp-*` file is left behind holding a snippet that was never
+acknowledged to any caller; it is inert and is not cleaned up (no requirement asks for it, and
+sweeping it would mean enumerating the directory at startup, which FR-038 forbids).
+
+## Read protocol
+
+One `read_bytes()` of `<DIR>/<id>.json`, then decode and validate. `FileNotFoundError` → `404`.
+Any other `OSError`, a decode failure, or a validation failure → `500`.
+
+## Delete protocol
+
+Read and validate the record, check expiry, compare the presented token with
+`secrets.compare_digest`, then a single `os.unlink`. A reader that already opened the file keeps
+reading the unlinked inode to completion, so a concurrent read returns the whole snippet or `404`,
+never a fragment (FR-043). `FileNotFoundError` on the unlink means another deletion won the race →
+`404`.
+
+## Assumptions
+
+POSIX semantics on a single local filesystem: `link` is atomic and fails on an existing destination,
+and an unlinked open file remains readable. The spec's Assumptions already exclude network shares
+with unusual semantics and other processes modifying the directory.
